<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacro Catino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacro Catino is a green hexagonal dish kept in the treasury of San Lorenzo Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Genoese crusaders brought it back from the capture of Caesarea in 1101, believing it to be carved from a single emerald, and it was later identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>. It was broken when returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops had taken it there, and it proved to be made of glass.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacro-catino.jpg" alt="Sacro Catino" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relic_of_the_Last_Supper_-_Genoa_Cathedral_-_Genoa_2014.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
